{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 39, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III was born around 1140 and succeeded his father as count at the age of 12.\n\n**Early Life and Reign**\nRaymond III was raised in the Kingdom of Jerusalem and received a good education. He was known for his intelligence and bravery. In 1152 Raymond II was murdered and Raymond III succeeded him as count. His mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age.\n\nRaymond III faced many challenges during his reign including the threat of the Crusader states being conquered by the Muslims. In 1157 he was captured by the Muslims while leading a campaign against them. He was released after a ransom was paid.\n\n**Marriage and Family**\nRaymond III married Eschiva of Bures in 1174. She was the daughter of William of Bures and the widow of Walter of Saint Omer. The marriage was likely arranged to strengthen the alliance between the County of Tripoli and the Principality of Galilee.\n\nRaymond III and Eschiva had no children together. However Raymond III had an illegitimate son named Raymond Rupen who was born around 1175. Raymond Rupen would go on to play an important role in the history of the County of Tripoli.\n\n**Relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem**\nRaymond III had a complex relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem. In 1174 he was appointed regent of the kingdom by King Baldwin IV who was suffering from leprosy. Raymond III played an important role in the kingdom's government and helped to defend it against the Muslims.\n\nHowever Raymond III's relationship with the kingdom was not without controversy. He was accused of plotting against King Baldwin IV and was forced to flee the kingdom. He later returned and was reconciled with the king.\n\nRaymond III died on September 20 1187 during the B